 - **[SPEC-SPECLINT-GATE-SIGNAL-001](.moai/specs/SPEC-SPECLINT-GATE-SIGNAL-001/spec.md)** — sync-phase close (3-phase plan→run→sync, card t525, Tier M). **The `SPEC Lint` CI gate on `develop` was permanently red, and a single standing non-advisory warning made it mathematically impossible for the gate to signal a genuine regression** — the person who broke something and the person who changed nothing saw the same red. `moai spec lint` gains a per-rule, non-advisory-warning baseline ratchet: `--baseline <path>` gates on the CHANGE against a checked-in JSON baseline rather than on the standing inventory. Error-severity findings always fail regardless of the baseline (`REQ-SLGS-009`); a rule's non-advisory warning count is blocked only when it rises above its recorded value, named with its delta (`ERROR-GATED` vs `EXCEEDED` in the output); a decrease passes and is reported without silently rewriting the baseline file; and the standing warning total stays visible in the output even on a green run. Rebaselining is an explicit, auditable action — `--update-baseline` requires a non-empty `--reason "<text>"` and records the tree SHA and date; an empty or missing reason is rejected and the baseline file is left untouched. `--strict` is unchanged and remains available for non-CI callers, but the two flags gate the same dimension and are mutually rejected. `.github/workflows/spec-lint.yml` now runs the baseline-gated invocation on `develop`/`main` pushes and ordinary pull requests (with a release-snapshot and a same-inputs-unchanged carve-out that runs an error-only lint instead), and the checked-in `.moai/spec-lint-baseline.json` is itself a lint-gate trigger path so a baseline-only commit is still checked. Separately, `SPEC-V3R4-CC2X-ADOPT-001` and `SPEC-V3R4-CC2X-ADOPT-002` — two umbrella research documents that were deliberately never meant to carry a `spec.md` — were relocated with their git history preserved to `.moai/research/SPEC-V3R4-CC2X-ADOPT-001-research.md` and `.moai/research/SPEC-V3R4-CC2X-ADOPT-002-research.md`, closing the `SpecsDirMissingSpecFile` findings the linter had raised against directories that were never going to gain a `spec.md`. Every rejected baseline flag combination — `--baseline` with `--strict`, a missing baseline file, a blank `--reason`, `--json` or `--sarif` with `--baseline` — now prints a one-line reason to stderr before exiting 3, rather than exiting 3 silently. 12 acceptance criteria; one of them (the CI-log half of `AC-SLGS-011`, reading the green CI run's inventory line) can only be observed after this branch lands on `develop` and is deferred to that verification.
+- **[SPEC-REVIEW-SECRET-SCAN-REFS-001](.moai/specs/SPEC-REVIEW-SECRET-SCAN-REFS-001/spec.md)** — sync-phase close (3-phase plan→run→sync, card t629, Tier M). **The `/moai review` secrets scan now covers commits that HEAD does not reach.** The incremental scan anchored its checkpoint on a single HEAD SHA (`<last-sha>..HEAD`) and claimed the same coverage over time as a full-history scan; a throwaway-fixture reproduction refuted that claim — a line committed on a branch HEAD does not reach, and never merged, was missed and stayed missed as the checkpoint advanced. The review workflow now prescribes a **per-ref tip store**, `.moai/state/secrets-scan-tips.txt`: before the scan starts, the tip of every ref is recorded as one caret-prefixed object name per line (`git for-each-ref --format='^%(objectname)'`); the store reaches the scan through `git log -p --all ... --stdin` as negated revisions, so every commit reachable from any ref or from HEAD that no recorded tip reaches is scanned; and the store is replaced only after the scan that carries the final result exits 0. A commit landing while the scan runs falls to the next review instead of being skipped. A recorded tip that no longer exists (`bad object` in the scan's error output) runs the full-history scan in its place; any other non-zero exit is reported as a scan failure and leaves the store unchanged. Two limits stay stated in the document: commits no ref and no HEAD reaches, such as commits reachable only through a reflog, are outside every scan step, and a line that only a merge commit's own changes introduce, such as a conflict resolution, is reported by neither scan. **Exact example-value allowlist**: a match is suppressed only when the matched text equals a listed value character for character; the list holds one publicly published example value (the example access key ID AWS publishes in its documentation), written as its full-length git blob digest so the document holds no line the scan's own regex matches. No path is excluded from any scan step, and a value that differs from the listed one by a single character is still reported. Documentation-only: both copies of the workflow — `.claude/skills/moai/workflows/review.md` and its byte-identical template mirror `internal/template/templates/.claude/skills/moai/workflows/review.md` — plus the regenerated `moai` hash line in `internal/template/catalog.yaml`; no Go source changed. The procedure was measured before it was worded: a three-cell measure-first gate (two fixture cells and one timing cell on this repository, each recorded `trustworthy`) preceded the edit. 16 acceptance criteria (15 PASS; AC-012 not applicable, since no gate cell was untrustworthy). The docs-site `/moai review` page still describes the former single-SHA checkpoint in all four locales; updating it is a separate step.
 - **[SPEC-TOOLPOLICY-DRIFT-GUARD-001](.moai/specs/SPEC-TOOLPOLICY-DRIFT-GUARD-001/spec.md)** — sync-phase close (3-phase plan→run→sync, card t619, Tier M). **`.moai/config/sections/tool-policy.yaml` had drifted from the enforced `.claude/settings.json` permissions block, and nothing measured the gap.** The YAML declared 12 `Write`/`Grep`/`Glob` secret-path denies and 1 `MultiEdit` allow that were never enforced, while settings.json enforced 7 allows (`CronCreate`/`CronDelete`/`CronList`/`EnterPlanMode`/`ExitPlanMode`/`EnterWorktree`/`ExitWorktree`) the YAML never declared — 20 differing specifiers in total, with no check to catch it. The YAML was reconciled to settings.json; settings.json itself stayed byte-identical, so no enforced permission changed. A read-only drift check (`internal/config/toolpolicy/drift_check_test.go`, set comparison of YAML-derived allow/deny against settings.json, fail-closed on missing/unparseable/empty/duplicate/overlapping input) now runs as `make tool-policy-drift-check`, wired as a prerequisite of `make build`, and CI's `go_code` path filter now includes `.claude/settings.json` so a settings-only change also triggers Go tests. False "structurally prevents drift" claims in the YAML header comment and in `internal/config/toolpolicy/types.go`'s package and `Metadata` doc comments were corrected to name the drift check as the actual mechanism. 10 acceptance criteria; one (AC-TDG-009) is recorded FAIL, attributed to a pre-existing `internal/cli` test broken by an earlier unrelated change and tracked separately.
 - **[SPEC-GO-TOOLCHAIN-SEC-002](.moai/specs/SPEC-GO-TOOLCHAIN-SEC-002/spec.md)** — sync-phase close (3-phase plan→run→sync, card t610, Tier S). **Go build baseline raised from go1.26.4 to go1.26.8 (security maintenance).** `govulncheck ./...` no longer reports the 8 standard-library advisories it reported against go1.26.4, each with a call path from this module's code to the affected standard-library symbol — GO-2026-6218, GO-2026-6091, GO-2026-6090, GO-2026-6089, GO-2026-6088, GO-2026-5972, GO-2026-5856, GO-2026-5026 — once the `go` directive on `go.mod:3` is raised to `go 1.26.8`; this entry does not claim exploitability of, or that any shipped binary was vulnerable under, go1.26.4 — only that the standard-library findings are no longer reported against the bumped toolchain. The toolchain is fetched automatically via `GOTOOLCHAIN=auto` reading the `go.mod` directive, so most environments need no local install; an environment pinned to `GOTOOLCHAIN=local` with an older installed Go needs Go 1.26.8 installed to build this module. 8 acceptance criteria. This sync commit carries the 3-phase close (`spec.md` frontmatter `in-progress → completed`, `status` + `updated` only) and the `progress.md` §E.4 signal.
 - **[SPEC-GATE-OXLINT-DETECT-001](.moai/specs/SPEC-GATE-OXLINT-DETECT-001/spec.md)** — sync-phase close (3-phase plan→run→sync, card t550, Tier M). **`moai gate` now detects oxlint on the Node lint axis** (#1631). A Node project whose only linter is oxlint matched neither of the two configured lint entries, so its lint axis ran **zero** steps while the gate still exited 0 — a pass that had checked nothing. The Node toolchain now carries a third config-gated entry that runs `npx oxlint` when any of oxlint's own discovery files is present: `.oxlintrc.json`, `.oxlintrc.jsonc`, `oxlint.config.ts`, `oxlint.config.mts`. The entry is optional and config-gated exactly like its siblings, so **the existing `eslint` and `biome` entries are unchanged** — an eslint project still runs eslint alone, a biome project biome alone, and a Node project carrying no linter config still runs zero lint steps and still exits 0. All seven fixtures were measured before and after the change; the linter-free scaffold is the control and did not move. Known limitation, closed knowingly rather than overlooked: a **zero-config** oxlint project presents no config file to gate on, so it still runs no lint step (`spec.md` §5). 9 acceptance criteria.
 - **Codex/Claude instruction contract alignment** — `moai init --llm claude|codex|both` replaces the ambiguous `--agent` selector, and the obsolete `moai update --add-codex` compatibility flag is removed in favor of the single additive command `moai tool enable codex`. The local launcher now reads an optional project-root `AGENTS.local.md` and injects its exact content into the Codex session as `developer_instructions`; `CLAUDE.local.md`, `.claude/settings.local.json`, and Claude automatic memory remain Claude-only, and shared `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md` no longer import local Codex guidance. The cross-harness contract also resolves the remote default branch dynamically, removes circular source-of-truth wording, aligns Tier S/M direct versus Tier L/`--pr` routing, and guards the root-plus-nested template discovery chain against Codex's 32 KiB default project-instruction limit.
