 - **[SPEC-CODEX-HOME-BACKSLASH-001](.moai/specs/SPEC-CODEX-HOME-BACKSLASH-001/spec.md)** — sync-phase close (3-phase plan→run→sync, card t571, Tier S). **A backslash-bearing path was refused in one declaration form and accepted in the other, and the accepting side is a deletion path.** `classifyCodexSkillPath` (`internal/cli/doctor_codex.go`) stripped the `~/` prefix *before* testing for a backslash, so `~/x\SKILL.md` never reached the oddly-formed refusal: it was classified home-relative, expanded, stat'ed, and — because a backslash is an ordinary filename byte on a `/`-separator host, so the expanded path does not exist — reached `Eligible=true` in `judgeCodexSkillEntry`. `Eligible` is what `pruneCodexSkillEntries` acts on, and it removes that entry's line range from the user's `~/.codex/config.toml`. The identical backslash written as a *relative* declaration (`x\SKILL.md`) was refused correctly. **The asymmetry was the whole defect**, and the write side had already been defending against it: `upsertCodexSkillDisable` refuses any backslash-bearing path on a `/`-separator host (`codex_skills_disable.go:261`), citing the same hazard in its own comment. Only the read/prune side was open. **The repair is a branch reordering, not new logic** — the backslash check moves above the `~/` peel and stays below `filepath.IsAbs`, giving `IsAbs → backslash → ~/~/ peel → residual ~ → relative`. `IsAbs` stays first so a native Windows `C:\...` absolute is still classified by what it is. **The check runs on the declaration, never on the expansion**: the expanded home is legitimately backslash-bearing on Windows (`C:\Users\x`), so moving the check below expansion — the alternative repair — would refuse every entry there; `TestJudgeCodexSkillEntryBackslashHomeStillExpands` pins that by asserting the observed stat argument rather than a call count, since a count of 1 is produced identically when the home stub never takes effect. **Exactly one classification cell changes**; POSIX-absolute-with-backslash, `~user`, plain relative, and clean home-relative are pinned unchanged by `TestCodexSkillPathPreservedShapes`. The binding guard asserts that the two declaration forms receive the *same* verdict, not merely that one of them is refused — a one-sided assertion would pass for the wrong reason if a later change made every shape oddly-formed. Two mutants were injected and observed red, one at a time in an exclusive window: restoring the old ordering reddens the symmetry guard (and, inherited, the stat-count-zero assertion), and hoisting the backslash check above `IsAbs` reddens the POSIX-absolute cell and no other. Package regression on `./internal/cli/...` is exit 0 with the SKIP set identical to the pre-change baseline in name and count (30). **Out of scope, declared rather than omitted:** a home-relative declaration escaping the home via `..` (`~/../../etc/x`) reaches the same deletion verdict through `filepath.Join`'s `Clean`, carries no backslash, and therefore survives this change untouched — a separate follow-up owns it.
+- **[SPEC-SYNC-GATE-FAILSTATE-001](.moai/specs/SPEC-SYNC-GATE-FAILSTATE-001/spec.md)** — sync-phase close (3-phase plan→run→sync, card t624, Tier M). **A failure recorded by the sync-phase quality gate disappeared on the very next turn on the same HEAD**: the Stop hook wrote only the HEAD SHA to `.moai/state/sync-quality-gate.last`, before the checks ran, so any later invocation on that HEAD short-circuited and exited silently — a broken build blocked exactly once, then passed with no check and no output on every subsequent turn. The hook now keeps a closed-token outcome record (`<sha> running|pass|fail`) plus two auxiliary files: a payload holding the exact stdout of a failing run (block or advisory), and a retry marker. A stored block is now re-delivered byte-identical while the mode resolves blocking, deferred (not dropped) when stdin carries `stop_hook_active`, and never re-delivered under an advisory resolution or as an advisory-first-run retroactive block. A `running` record younger than the hook's own registered timeout (60s) gets a non-blocking notice instead of a re-run; older than that, one re-run is allowed per HEAD, after which further turns get a non-blocking notice instead of falling silent — so repeated notices never consume the runtime Stop-hook block cap. Deleting the record (or `.moai/state` entirely) is still the only way to force a fresh gate; no new flag or environment variable was added. Separately, the sync workflow document claimed "the dependency vulnerability scan runs automatically via the Stop hook" — that claim is false; the hook only observes whether the HEAD commit changed a manifest of the detected language and records that as informational `deps_modified`, never a decision input. The hook's own header and inline comments used the narrower but still-inaccurate label "dependency manifest audit" for the same non-decision-input observation. Both copies of the document and both copies of the hook are corrected, and the document now states that Phase 8's CRITICAL-only stop gate is an additional lens that never clears an earlier sync-auditor Critical/High FAIL. 15 acceptance criteria (AC-001..AC-015, per `acceptance.md` §D), all PASS; all 27 named mutants (§D.16) turned their targeted rows red, including a mid-run amendment (AC-013 rows S2/S3) added after mutant M18 first survived against the original AC-013 rows. Evidence: `.moai/reports/t624/`. **Known remaining inaccuracy, tracked rather than fixed here:** other project surfaces still describe this hook as running "lint + test + coverage delta" — out of this SPEC's approved scope, tracked by the lead as follow-up card t644. This sync commit carries the 3-phase close (`spec.md` frontmatter `in-progress → completed`, `status` + `updated` only), the `progress.md` §E.4 signal, and this entry; `sync_commit_sha` is written as the `pending-backfill` placeholder in this commit — a commit cannot cite its own hash — and the resolved value is backfilled in a following commit. 🗿 MoAI
 
